retly  inserting  trackable microchips into the vaccines  so i went back to my dad and i said dad  i'm sorry i worry about you  like that's just nuts and he goes well  you never know  like a lot of things have happened in my  lifespan i could never have imagined  when i was a child  so i said to dad the first thing you  need to do dad when one of your friends  sends you one of these things is look at  where it's from  but when hatley searched for the video's  source she was overwhelmed  by the sheer number of sites and social  media accounts  spreading the claims yeah i everyone's  confused this is huge  you know and if they hear a strong voice  they tend to latch onto it and then  follow it right down a rabbit hole  hatley is also the district chair of the  ontario medical association  the oma just commissioned a study to see  how widespread these conspiracies have  become  they found 105 000 ontarians discussing  the pandemic as a hoax  with about three quarters spreading  misinformation  and that's confusing for the population  and then you add to that  the fact that we really haven't had a  strong  individual in canada to provide  information to canadian citizens with a  single voice  they've been hearing from voices all  over the place  [Music]  people are afraid and there's a  continuous sense of disaster  university of waterloo professor carmen  celastini is an expert in conspiracies  and says the pandemic has produced a  plethora of different theories  which tend to follow a similar theme  they believe  that using the laws around covid and  isolation and keeping us home  is the catalyst to a cabal starting a  new world order  while the stories might sound silly  celestini says their membership is  growing  exponentially the pandemic has produced  a perfect storm  of fear frustration and isolation  with more time spent online it's not  people who are uneducated or people who  are you know on the lowest end of the  social economic  tiers it's people everywhere that are  being  brought into this and they're afraid  they're you know they don't have jobs  they're alone  so they're turning to communities on the  internet  [Music]  susan says she's witnessed that  transformation  up close she asked us not to reveal her  identity because she lives at home with  her mother  afraid she could be kicked out for  speaking up  to learn that my mom was like that it  was a shock  growing up she says her mother was her  hero a successful  award-winning businesswoman with no  history of conspiracy beliefs  before the pandemic she was very mad  she's someone who was really social so  the idea of social distancing and not  seeing people  it was making her suffer susan says her  mother who never used computers before  the pandemic  started spending a lot of time online  and came across an open letter written  by a controversial catholic archbishop  claiming covet 19 was a global  conspiracy  against god and humanity she told me  we're in world war iii people are  suffering for nothing  that's when really things started to get  really really crazy  she says her mother started attending  protests claiming the pandemic was a  hoax  and refusing to stay home or wear a mask  when susan tried challenging those  beliefs she says her mother  threatened to kick her out of the house  there was no more relationship at that  point  at the end of the conversation i told  her that i missed her  what do you mean by that that she she  isn't the same person  it's like someone else was in the house  who looked like my mom and sounded like  her  but it's not the person i remember do  you feel like you've lost some piece of  her  yeah she's gone  there are many canadians who have family  members  um who are falling for for  stories or theories that just seem so  far-fetched and kyle matthews warns the  consequences  reach far beyond family feuds the expert  in online extremism at concordia  university  is now part of a team investigating how  covet conspiraci